00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:08.970
RAVI KIRAN KUMAR: What
is Cloud Identity?

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:12.300
It's a cloud-based identity
and access management

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:15.740
solution and a device
management solution.

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:18.720
So you have two
solutions packaged

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:22.260
into one single
product, Cloud Identity.

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:25.140
So it gives you a single
pane of glass for you

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:29.220
to manage all your
users and devices

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:34.140
and also applications, all
from one single pane of glass.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:37.200
And it also brings
BeyondCorp security model

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:38.950
to your enterprises.

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:42.420
How many of you have heard or
know about BeyondCorp security

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:44.211
model?

00:00:44.211 --> 00:00:46.780
OK, about 30% of the room.

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:49.240
So I won't go very
deep into BeyondCorp

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:51.940
because it takes
the entire session.

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:55.430
But I wanted to give you a quick
one-minute intro on BeyondCorp,

00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:57.700
because that's very important.

00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:00.940
BeyondCorp is the
next-generation security model

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:04.030
that Google conceived
a few years ago.

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:09.460
And there are two key tenets
based on from a BeyondCorp

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:11.590
security model perspective.

00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:15.690
The first one is
no-trust networks.

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:18.070
You're not even trusting
your own network.

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:21.820
What we do is we inspect
each and every request that's

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:26.560
coming to your
applications, and we

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:31.990
make sure that these requests
are authenticated, authorized,

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:34.210
and also encrypted.

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:37.000
And the second key thing from
a BeyondCorp security model

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:40.840
perspective is that we go beyond
the traditional identity-based

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:41.500
access.

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:44.770
So we also look at the
context of the request,

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:47.470
where the request is coming
from, what kind of device

00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:48.400
you're using.

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:51.640
Are you using a company-owned
device or a BYOD device?

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:54.320
What kind of security posture
do you have on the device?

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:56.200
So there are a lot of
things that we look

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:58.300
at from a context perspective.

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:01.690
And then you'll be able to
create these granular access

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:03.920
control permissions
on your application.

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:05.950
So that's the BeyondCorp.

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:08.919
So Cloud Identity
is the vehicle that

00:02:08.919 --> 00:02:12.790
brings BeyondCorp security
model for your IT and business

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:14.410
applications.

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:17.830
And also, Cloud Identity
is the identity layer

00:02:17.830 --> 00:02:21.070
that powers G Suite
and GCP applications.

00:02:21.070 --> 00:02:23.200
It's been there for some time.

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:25.420
We have hundreds of
thousands of customers

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:29.680
who are using Cloud Identity,
about billions of users.

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:33.430
And it's also offered as a
standalone product starting

00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:38.780
March of last year in 2018.

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:40.990
And when you look
at Cloud Identity,

00:02:40.990 --> 00:02:43.130
it includes all
the key components

00:02:43.130 --> 00:02:47.240
that you normally look for
in a full-fledged identity

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:50.690
and access management
platform, like user lifecycle

00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:54.680
management, account security,
multi-factor authentication,

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:57.080
single sign-on into your
internal applications,

00:02:57.080 --> 00:02:59.660
external applications,
third-party SaaS-based

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:03.710
applications, APIs
for extensibility,

00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:06.860
and reporting, auditing for
your compliance purposes,

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:12.800
and most importantly, the open
standards adherence-- so SAML,

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:17.240
OpenID Connect, OAuth,
[? Skim, ?] and whatnot.

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:20.810
And now you may ask,
hey, what about some

00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:22.810
of the on-premise
applications that I have?

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:32.100
So we recognize that the
SaaS-based application adoption

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:35.700
is going through the roof for
small business, medium, large.

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:38.160
Regardless of what's the
size of your business,

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:41.130
everyone is using
SaaS-based applications.

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:44.790
But like Sundar said
yesterday in the keynote, most

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:46.770
of the large
companies, they still

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:48.990
have majority of
their applications

00:03:48.990 --> 00:03:53.580
and infrastructure on-premise
for a variety of reasons.

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:55.890
So you may probably
need some more time

00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:59.820
to migrate, or
retire, or modernize.

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:03.630
But what happens is, in
this particular case,

00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:07.080
giving your employees
a way to access

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:10.410
these modern applications
and legacy applications

00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:13.200
in a simple way is
really challenging.

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:16.649
And it forces your IT teams
to start maintaining these two

00:04:16.649 --> 00:04:18.240
different identity systems--

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:22.650
one for modern applications, one
for on-premise applications--

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:25.800
which increases your cost
and, most importantly,

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:27.240
the complexity.

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:29.040
So for those of
you who said, we're

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:31.860
using Active Directory, for
you Active Directory admins,

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:34.080
you all know how
complex that could be.

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:36.360
So you have hundreds
of servers, probably--

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:38.820
dozens of hundreds of servers,
depending upon your company

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:39.690
size--

00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:42.060
and replication
topologies, trusts.

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:45.370
You need to set up disaster
recovery, clustering.

00:04:45.370 --> 00:04:46.630
So it's complex, right?

00:04:46.630 --> 00:04:49.560
So as your company grows,
the whole infrastructure

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:52.630
becomes very, very complex.

00:04:52.630 --> 00:04:54.360
So what's the deal?

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:57.570
So we have these two systems--

00:04:57.570 --> 00:05:00.120
very costly, and then complex.

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:02.650
How to deal with that issue?

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:04.620
That's where Cloud
Identity hybrid approach

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:06.480
comes into picture.

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:10.620
It has the components that
helps you meet where you are.

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:14.020
We recognize you have these
on-premise applications.

00:05:14.020 --> 00:05:15.750
And probably, you
may need more time

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:19.320
to migrate and modernize
those applications

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:21.180
or retire those applications.

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:23.280
Or in some cases, you
may want to continue

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:25.410
to use it for whatever reason.

00:05:25.410 --> 00:05:29.790
What we want to do is we
want to provide the bridge

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:32.490
to connect all those
on-premise-based applications,

00:05:32.490 --> 00:05:35.670
give a versatile solution
so that you can use

00:05:35.670 --> 00:05:40.530
one single solution to integrate
your SaaS-based applications,

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:44.040
web applications, and also
on-premise-based applications.

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:46.140
What it does is
it basically helps

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:50.130
you reduce the cost of ownership
and also, most importantly,

00:05:50.130 --> 00:05:52.500
reducing the complexity
that's associated

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:55.560
with on-premise
enterprise directories.

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:57.480
And then finally,
when you're ready--

00:05:57.480 --> 00:05:59.460
for example, after
some time when

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:01.950
you're ready to move
some of your applications

00:06:01.950 --> 00:06:04.110
and infrastructure
and move it to cloud.

00:06:04.110 --> 00:06:06.360
Maybe you're doing
a lift-and-shift.

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:09.480
The key thing is you don't
have to make any changes that

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:10.900
are related to identity.

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:13.530
So it'll be a seamless
transformation-- so one

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:14.670
less thing to worry about.

00:06:17.660 --> 00:06:20.180
So now let's zoom
into Secure LDAP.

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:23.240
As I said, so this is one of the
key component in Cloud Identity

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:25.460
hybrid approach.

00:06:25.460 --> 00:06:27.900
What it does is it's
an LDAP-as-a-service.

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:31.710
So imagine you have a bunch
of LDAP servers in the cloud,

00:06:31.710 --> 00:06:33.593
except you don't
have to set it up.

00:06:33.593 --> 00:06:34.760
You don't have to manage it.

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:36.030
You don't have to patch it.

00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:40.130
It's all done for you because
it's a managed service.

00:06:40.130 --> 00:06:42.590
So what it does is it
actually takes your Google

00:06:42.590 --> 00:06:45.980
directory, your users,
groups, everything, and then

00:06:45.980 --> 00:06:48.920
it basically makes it available
as a joint LDAP directory

00:06:48.920 --> 00:06:51.980
for your on-premise-based
applications, legacy

00:06:51.980 --> 00:06:55.490
applications, that
talk LDAP protocol.

00:06:55.490 --> 00:06:59.150
And if your applications are
talking to an LDAP directory

00:06:59.150 --> 00:07:03.620
for authentication,
authorization, user lookups,

00:07:03.620 --> 00:07:04.490
group lookups--

00:07:04.490 --> 00:07:07.650
no matter how you use
your LDAP applications,

00:07:07.650 --> 00:07:12.110
so this can be that LDAP
application in the cloud.

00:07:12.110 --> 00:07:15.620
And another key benefit is
regardless of where you have

00:07:15.620 --> 00:07:19.310
your applications-- it
could be on your on-premise,

00:07:19.310 --> 00:07:23.010
it could be in your private
cloud, public cloud, Amazon,

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:24.780
Google, Azure, it
doesn't matter--

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:27.200
so the access will
be very smooth.

00:07:30.690 --> 00:07:34.620
Now, let's look at a
pictorial representation.

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:36.690
So this is the most
simplified version

00:07:36.690 --> 00:07:39.390
of an on-premise infrastructure.

00:07:39.390 --> 00:07:41.340
So you have some
applications there.

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:45.980
You have IT infrastructure, like
VPN servers or RADIUS servers,

00:07:45.980 --> 00:07:48.480
network attached servers,
and then printers and things

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:49.000
like that.

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:51.563
And also, you have desktops.

00:07:51.563 --> 00:07:52.980
So traditionally,
they all connect

00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:56.280
to Active Directory, or one
of your LDAP directories

00:07:56.280 --> 00:07:57.870
like OpenLDAP.

00:07:57.870 --> 00:08:00.510
So what you can do
is, using Secure LDAP,

00:08:00.510 --> 00:08:02.357
you can go to
those applications.

00:08:02.357 --> 00:08:04.440
And then there, you can
onboard these applications

00:08:04.440 --> 00:08:06.000
into Cloud Identity
within minutes.

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:08.490
You'll be able to
see in a few minutes.

00:08:08.490 --> 00:08:09.840
You onboard those applications.

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:12.300
We generate digital
certificates for you.

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:15.450
And then you'll be able to go
to individual applications,

00:08:15.450 --> 00:08:19.020
upload the digital certificate,
go to the directory settings,

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:22.860
and just point those
applications to Secure

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:24.750
LDAP as your LDAP server.

00:08:24.750 --> 00:08:26.760
And that's it.

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:28.310
And the most
important thing here

00:08:28.310 --> 00:08:30.680
is no user change
management required.

00:08:30.680 --> 00:08:34.549
So if they're used to seeing
same kind of login pages,

00:08:34.549 --> 00:08:38.750
if you're logging into
SAP or a VPN solution--

00:08:38.750 --> 00:08:41.929
so they'll be seeing
the same login pages.

00:08:41.929 --> 00:08:45.020
So behind the scenes, instead
of your servers talking

00:08:45.020 --> 00:08:47.480
to an on-premise
Active Directory,

00:08:47.480 --> 00:08:50.120
they'll be talking to Secure
LDAP for authentication

00:08:50.120 --> 00:08:50.960
and directory needs.

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:55.110
So this is another scenario.

00:08:55.110 --> 00:08:57.330
So traditionally, when
you lift and shift

00:08:57.330 --> 00:09:02.040
some of those applications
onto the IaaS platforms,

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:05.752
so you generally throw in
some VMs for Active Directory

00:09:05.752 --> 00:09:07.710
because you want to
support those applications.

00:09:07.710 --> 00:09:10.380
And again, it's not
one or two servers.

00:09:10.380 --> 00:09:14.040
So probably, you will end
up setting up multiple VMs.

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:17.730
You need disaster recovery,
so you throw in more servers.

00:09:17.730 --> 00:09:20.310
And if you're a
large company, you

00:09:20.310 --> 00:09:22.910
have users in multiple regions.

00:09:22.910 --> 00:09:27.580
So now you copy and paste the
same setup in multiple regions.

00:09:27.580 --> 00:09:30.850
And then now, if you have
LDAP-based applications,

00:09:30.850 --> 00:09:33.070
you might also need load
balancers before that.

00:09:33.070 --> 00:09:36.390
So what you have done
is the complexity

00:09:36.390 --> 00:09:39.090
that you currently have
on-premise with your directory

00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:42.930
servers, you just replicated
the same kind of complexity

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:43.757
in the cloud.

00:09:43.757 --> 00:09:45.090
It's a double whammy now, right?

00:09:45.090 --> 00:09:49.180
So it became even more complex.

00:09:49.180 --> 00:09:53.130
So again, you can avoid all
that by simply integrating

00:09:53.130 --> 00:09:57.210
your cloud workloads
directly into Secure LDAP

00:09:57.210 --> 00:10:00.120
so that you don't need any
of those Active Directories

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:00.970
out there.

00:10:00.970 --> 00:10:03.810
However, so we
recognize that there

00:10:03.810 --> 00:10:05.670
are some applications
that explicitly

00:10:05.670 --> 00:10:07.330
need Active Directory--
for example,

00:10:07.330 --> 00:10:10.380
if you have Windows
servers, or maybe you

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:14.760
have a SQL server or SharePoint,
other Microsoft applications.

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:17.040
So for those reasons,
we've announced

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:20.340
today something called Cloud
AD, which is a managed AD

00:10:20.340 --> 00:10:21.510
service that you can use.

00:10:24.940 --> 00:10:27.910
So in summary, the
key benefits are

00:10:27.910 --> 00:10:31.120
you can use one identity
and access management system

00:10:31.120 --> 00:10:33.640
to integrate both your
modern applications

00:10:33.640 --> 00:10:36.010
and also legacy applications.

00:10:36.010 --> 00:10:38.620
And you can reduce
the dependence

00:10:38.620 --> 00:10:41.170
on on-premise
directories, which will

00:10:41.170 --> 00:10:44.860
help you reduce the cost
and also the complexity.

00:10:44.860 --> 00:10:47.980
And then finally, it gives
you the Google scale,

00:10:47.980 --> 00:10:52.210
Google performance, and also
Google-based reliability

00:10:52.210 --> 00:10:55.287
when you use Secure
LDAP and Cloud Identity.

00:10:58.090 --> 00:11:02.080
Secure LDAP is
generally available

00:11:02.080 --> 00:11:03.910
starting November of 2018.

00:11:03.910 --> 00:11:06.220
So we have a lot
of our customers

00:11:06.220 --> 00:11:08.800
who are already reaping the
benefits of Secure LDAP.

00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:10.930
It's available in
Cloud Identity Premium.

00:11:10.930 --> 00:11:16.570
If you are a GCP customer,
you start with the base Cloud

00:11:16.570 --> 00:11:17.110
Identity.

00:11:17.110 --> 00:11:21.070
All you have to do is add on
Cloud Identity Premium, which

00:11:21.070 --> 00:11:24.850
allows you to use Secure
LDAP and many other features

00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:28.330
that we discussed previously--
single sign-on and a bunch

00:11:28.330 --> 00:11:31.070
of other identity and
access management features.

00:11:31.070 --> 00:11:33.790
And if you're a
G Suite customer,

00:11:33.790 --> 00:11:37.060
if you have G Suite Enterprise,
all these capabilities

00:11:37.060 --> 00:11:38.920
are part of G Suite Enterprise.

00:11:38.920 --> 00:11:41.980
If you're using G Suite
Basic or Business,

00:11:41.980 --> 00:11:44.290
you can simply add on
Cloud Identity Premium

00:11:44.290 --> 00:11:46.940
on top of what you have.

00:11:46.940 --> 00:11:48.550
And if you're an
education customer,

00:11:48.550 --> 00:11:51.250
it's part of G
Suite for Education.

00:11:54.130 --> 00:11:56.900
These are some of the
Secure LDAP partners.

00:11:56.900 --> 00:11:59.710
These are the companies who
work with us very closely

00:11:59.710 --> 00:12:01.760
to integrate with Secure LDAP.

00:12:01.760 --> 00:12:06.280
And PaperCut is one of those
partners who are in this room.

00:12:06.280 --> 00:12:09.130
And these are not
the only applications

00:12:09.130 --> 00:12:10.930
that work with Secure LDAP.

00:12:10.930 --> 00:12:15.700
So you can pick any
LDAP-compliant application.

00:12:15.700 --> 00:12:18.430
You'll be able to
integrate with Secure LDAP.

