00:14:02.650 --> 00:14:05.830
All right, I got
my mouse corner.

00:14:05.830 --> 00:14:08.560
So you click on Selected
Organizational Units,

00:14:08.560 --> 00:14:11.800
and you can decide which
part of your directory

00:14:11.800 --> 00:14:13.870
you want to expose.

00:14:13.870 --> 00:14:18.340
And if this application is
used by your entire company,

00:14:18.340 --> 00:14:20.410
you may want to expose
your entire directory

00:14:20.410 --> 00:14:21.720
for authentication.

00:14:21.720 --> 00:14:24.010
And you can decide
these access controls

00:14:24.010 --> 00:14:28.120
not at the LDAP client level,
but for each transaction.

00:14:28.120 --> 00:14:31.000
So the first
transaction is verifying

00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:33.830
user credentials, which is
the authentication part.

00:14:33.830 --> 00:14:36.280
The next one is user lookups--

00:14:36.280 --> 00:14:36.890
same thing.

00:14:36.890 --> 00:14:39.610
So if you want to
allow this application

00:14:39.610 --> 00:14:42.220
to do user lookups for
your entire directory,

00:14:42.220 --> 00:14:44.305
you click on Entire Domain.

00:14:44.305 --> 00:14:46.180
And then you can also
decide whether you want

00:14:46.180 --> 00:14:49.840
to expose your groups or not.

00:14:49.840 --> 00:14:52.760
Now, I'm clicking
on Add LDAP Client.

00:14:52.760 --> 00:14:56.300
Within a few seconds, we will
onboard this application.

00:14:56.300 --> 00:15:00.500
And we're also generating
a digital certificate

00:15:00.500 --> 00:15:02.240
that can be used.

00:15:02.240 --> 00:15:07.710
And click on go to the
LDAP client details.

00:15:07.710 --> 00:15:09.985
And I'm switching
on this service.

00:15:22.490 --> 00:15:26.240
So once the application
is switched on,

00:15:26.240 --> 00:15:30.300
it's available to accept
requests from PaperCut.

00:15:30.300 --> 00:15:32.660
So now, the part two
of the configuration

00:15:32.660 --> 00:15:35.930
is you need to go
to PaperCut, and you

00:15:35.930 --> 00:15:39.890
point PaperCut to use Google
as your LDAP directory.

00:15:39.890 --> 00:15:41.720
So this is their admin console.

00:15:41.720 --> 00:15:45.290
Here, you'll be able to see
a bunch of LDAP directories.

00:15:45.290 --> 00:15:47.510
Select Google Cloud Directory.

00:15:47.510 --> 00:15:50.930
If you're configuring your
own LDAP applications,

00:15:50.930 --> 00:15:53.660
don't worry if you don't
see Google Cloud Directory.

00:15:53.660 --> 00:15:56.840
You can pick any generic
LDAP option there.

00:15:56.840 --> 00:15:59.910
OpenLDAP, That should work fine.

00:15:59.910 --> 00:16:03.920
And I've typed in
my domain name here.

00:16:03.920 --> 00:16:08.510
And then I'm uploading
the digital certificate.

00:16:12.110 --> 00:16:17.630
And the digital
certificate is uploaded.

00:16:17.630 --> 00:16:20.180
And I'm clicking
on Synchronize Now.

00:16:20.180 --> 00:16:22.370
What's happening
is-- so now, PaperCut

00:16:22.370 --> 00:16:26.240
is authenticating with Google
using this digital certificate

00:16:26.240 --> 00:16:27.830
that we just uploaded.

00:16:27.830 --> 00:16:29.690
And once it
authenticates, it was

00:16:29.690 --> 00:16:34.040
able to make calls and do
user lookups and group lookups

00:16:34.040 --> 00:16:35.180
using the LDAP protocol.

00:16:37.950 --> 00:16:40.660
And this is the admin use case.

00:16:40.660 --> 00:16:43.400
The other use case
is the end user.

00:16:43.400 --> 00:16:45.720
So they can walk up to any
multifunction printer that

00:16:45.720 --> 00:16:48.060
are managed by
PaperCut, and they can

00:16:48.060 --> 00:16:49.740
type in the Google credentials.

00:16:49.740 --> 00:16:53.860
They'll be able to release
all those print jobs.

00:16:53.860 --> 00:16:56.790
So that's the end user
use case for PaperCut.

00:16:59.370 --> 00:17:05.130
So if you've seen this, it took
less than about five minutes

00:17:05.130 --> 00:17:09.300
for us to configure PaperCut
or any other applications.

00:17:09.300 --> 00:17:12.060
All you need to do is make
sure you understand where to go

00:17:12.060 --> 00:17:16.140
and where to punch in the
details to point to Google.

00:17:16.140 --> 00:17:18.300
We have very detailed
documentation

00:17:18.300 --> 00:17:21.510
on how to configure
all the partner

00:17:21.510 --> 00:17:22.980
applications on our website.

00:17:22.980 --> 00:17:25.770
And we do have some
generic documentation

00:17:25.770 --> 00:17:27.930
if you have any other
LDAP application

00:17:27.930 --> 00:17:29.110
you want to configure.

00:17:29.110 --> 00:17:31.480
DECLAN HOGAN: Any of you
ever flown on AirAsia?

00:17:31.480 --> 00:17:32.710
No?

00:17:32.710 --> 00:17:33.880
Not you, Edward.

00:17:33.880 --> 00:17:35.320
[LAUGHS]

00:17:35.320 --> 00:17:40.180
OK, so let's get to the--

00:17:40.180 --> 00:17:43.510
so we were established
in Malaysia in 2001.

00:17:43.510 --> 00:17:49.780
Tony Fernandes bought an airline
for one Malaysian ringgit,

00:17:49.780 --> 00:17:52.960
which is about $0.20--

00:17:52.960 --> 00:17:54.100
$0.25?

00:17:54.100 --> 00:17:58.780
It got two planes and
$10 million of debt.

00:17:58.780 --> 00:18:02.700
So since then, we've put
on another 200 aircraft.

00:18:02.700 --> 00:18:06.960
And we carried about 90 to 100
million passengers this year.

00:18:06.960 --> 00:18:11.670
We have 120 destinations
or more-- fly everywhere

00:18:11.670 --> 00:18:18.780
from Jeddah in Saudi Arabia
to Hawaii and everywhere

00:18:18.780 --> 00:18:21.330
in between.

00:18:21.330 --> 00:18:23.620
And we have about 25,000 staff.

00:18:23.620 --> 00:18:27.840
And it's actually nine airlines
in five different countries--

00:18:27.840 --> 00:18:32.540
so Malaysia, Philippines,
India, Thailand, Indonesia,

00:18:32.540 --> 00:18:34.065
Japan-- so five or six.

00:18:34.065 --> 00:18:34.650
Oh, OK.

00:18:37.800 --> 00:18:38.400
So that's us.

00:18:41.120 --> 00:18:42.800
We are currently on
Active Directory.

00:18:42.800 --> 00:18:44.420
And it's on-prem
Active Directory.

00:18:44.420 --> 00:18:47.930
And each country
will have its own DC,

00:18:47.930 --> 00:18:51.350
and they will all sync together.

00:18:51.350 --> 00:18:52.950
They're all supposed
to sync together,

00:18:52.950 --> 00:18:54.620
but that's not
necessarily how it works.

00:18:57.400 --> 00:18:59.900
It is extremely
challenging to manage.

00:18:59.900 --> 00:19:02.540
And we really
wouldn't be changing

00:19:02.540 --> 00:19:05.990
if the auditors hadn't killed
me every time they do an audit.

00:19:05.990 --> 00:19:08.390
And when they do IT
general controls audits,

00:19:08.390 --> 00:19:11.930
they always find
people who exist in AD,

00:19:11.930 --> 00:19:14.030
who had left the company from--

00:19:14.030 --> 00:19:17.590
the HR system said
they left X days ago.

00:19:17.590 --> 00:19:20.150
They're either
still in AD, or they

00:19:20.150 --> 00:19:23.660
were removed from AD
days, weeks, months

00:19:23.660 --> 00:19:25.290
after they left the company.

00:19:25.290 --> 00:19:30.320
So that's a security
challenge, an audit challenge.

00:19:30.320 --> 00:19:33.020
And it's just too
hard to manage.

00:19:33.020 --> 00:19:37.510
We have PowerShell scripts
running between HR applications

00:19:37.510 --> 00:19:40.960
and AD to pick up users,
configure them in AD,

00:19:40.960 --> 00:19:43.390
push them to multiple
other systems

00:19:43.390 --> 00:19:45.160
that they need access to.

00:19:45.160 --> 00:19:49.330
And it's by the grace of God
if that PowerShell script

00:19:49.330 --> 00:19:55.700
works, doesn't work, fails,
fails at a particular record.

00:19:55.700 --> 00:19:57.620
HR may have given the
person a different email

00:19:57.620 --> 00:20:01.640
address in the HR system
than we give in AD.

00:20:01.640 --> 00:20:04.190
It's just too hard to manage.

00:20:08.190 --> 00:20:09.900
Why did we go with
Cloud Identity?

00:20:09.900 --> 00:20:12.643
We didn't really go with Cloud
Identity, we went with Google.

00:20:12.643 --> 00:20:18.840
So we're migrating
all our apps onto GCP.

00:20:18.840 --> 00:20:22.920
And we currently have
on-prem, colo, private cloud,

00:20:22.920 --> 00:20:28.830
SaaS, AWS, Azure, Alibaba.

00:20:28.830 --> 00:20:31.690
We have applications everywhere.

00:20:31.690 --> 00:20:33.660
That sounds like
a very enlightened

00:20:33.660 --> 00:20:37.260
multi-cloud strategy,
but it's not.

00:20:37.260 --> 00:20:39.570
It has evolved that way.

00:20:39.570 --> 00:20:44.940
And yeah, it's too
much to manage.

00:20:44.940 --> 00:20:49.800
And we have 215-odd
applications that

00:20:49.800 --> 00:20:52.770
are spread over all of
those hosting methods.

00:20:52.770 --> 00:20:53.490
So yeah.

00:20:53.490 --> 00:20:55.978
Anybody else got
similar environment?

00:20:55.978 --> 00:20:57.890
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:58.447 --> 00:20:59.780
A couple of hands went up there.

00:20:59.780 --> 00:21:00.280
Yeah, OK.

00:21:00.280 --> 00:21:00.780
Good.

00:21:07.430 --> 00:21:10.832
And when you saw, it was
reduced dependency on AD.

00:21:10.832 --> 00:21:12.380
We don't want to
reduce dependency,

00:21:12.380 --> 00:21:14.720
we want to get
rid of it entirely

00:21:14.720 --> 00:21:16.380
out of our organization.

00:21:16.380 --> 00:21:18.530
We want to get off
our enterprise license

00:21:18.530 --> 00:21:19.640
with Microsoft.

00:21:19.640 --> 00:21:24.140
We have got Gmail, G Suite
for every single user.

00:21:24.140 --> 00:21:26.880
We are pushing everybody
onto Chrome devices.

00:21:26.880 --> 00:21:28.880
We're even contemplating
turning Windows devices

00:21:28.880 --> 00:21:29.720
into Chrome devices.

00:21:29.720 --> 00:21:31.178
And we'll have a
crack at Neverware

00:21:31.178 --> 00:21:34.760
after this conference.

00:21:34.760 --> 00:21:37.000
I see a couple of guys
from the data team.

00:21:37.000 --> 00:21:38.990
So there-- boys.

00:21:38.990 --> 00:21:44.330
So they're big GCP
users, big Data Studio.

00:21:44.330 --> 00:21:45.770
They love all the Google tools.

00:21:45.770 --> 00:21:47.270
So we're pushing
the whole company.

00:21:47.270 --> 00:21:49.340
So it's an enterprise-wide
transformation

00:21:49.340 --> 00:21:55.045
to get people off the Microsoft
stack onto the Google stack.

00:21:55.045 --> 00:21:56.420
And we're in a
hurry to get there

00:21:56.420 --> 00:21:59.940
because our enterprise
agreement expires next year.

00:21:59.940 --> 00:22:02.890
So we want everything on GCP,
everything authenticating

00:22:02.890 --> 00:22:09.930
through Cloud Identity, every
user getting off Microsoft

00:22:09.930 --> 00:22:14.640
Office products by
the end of this year,

00:22:14.640 --> 00:22:17.880
so that we're not at the edge
of a cliff when our enterprise

00:22:17.880 --> 00:22:18.630
agreement expires.

00:22:22.520 --> 00:22:23.480
Let's see.

00:22:23.480 --> 00:22:23.980
OK.

00:22:23.980 --> 00:22:27.820
Well actually, I will go back.

00:22:27.820 --> 00:22:30.920
And the one biggest
challenge there--

00:22:30.920 --> 00:22:34.360
Microsoft Excel.

00:22:34.360 --> 00:22:36.610
That is absolutely
the one challenge.

00:22:36.610 --> 00:22:38.980
The rest of moving
clouds, lift and shift,

00:22:38.980 --> 00:22:42.460
building native on GCP,
that's all quite easy.

00:22:42.460 --> 00:22:44.770
Using Cloud Identity and
spinning up applications

00:22:44.770 --> 00:22:49.822
and pointing them at Cloud
Identity, that's easy enough.

00:22:49.822 --> 00:22:51.780
Getting rid of AD, that's
actually not too bad.

00:22:51.780 --> 00:22:57.310
But getting people to give up
their Microsoft Excel is like--

00:22:57.310 --> 00:23:00.390
is-- we have a team
who all they do

00:23:00.390 --> 00:23:02.550
is go around, trying
to kill Excel.

00:23:02.550 --> 00:23:03.900
And they make people cry.

00:23:03.900 --> 00:23:06.880
They make grown
adults cry every week.

00:23:06.880 --> 00:23:09.120
[LAUGHTER]

00:23:09.120 --> 00:23:10.620
So don't try and do that first.

00:23:10.620 --> 00:23:12.200
Leave that till last.

00:23:12.200 --> 00:23:14.040
[LAUGHTER]

00:23:14.040 --> 00:23:15.700
OK.

00:23:15.700 --> 00:23:16.200
Yeah.

00:23:16.200 --> 00:23:18.890
So cloud native-- we
want everything on GCP

00:23:18.890 --> 00:23:20.240
because we have too many clouds.

00:23:20.240 --> 00:23:24.470
And we have too much stuff
spread all over the place,

00:23:24.470 --> 00:23:26.960
and we just want to consolidate
on one single cloud.

00:23:26.960 --> 00:23:28.910
We're not too worried
about whether we

00:23:28.910 --> 00:23:31.520
need a dual cloud for DR.

00:23:31.520 --> 00:23:34.100
If Google loses
the world, then we

00:23:34.100 --> 00:23:35.450
will be in trouble on that day.

00:23:35.450 --> 00:23:37.940
But right now, if
somebody digs up

00:23:37.940 --> 00:23:39.690
a piece of fiber
outside of our building,

00:23:39.690 --> 00:23:41.540
we're in bigger trouble.

00:23:41.540 --> 00:23:43.732
And these two are
laughing because that

00:23:43.732 --> 00:23:44.690
has happened last year.

00:23:47.320 --> 00:23:49.410
Yeah, so it's not so
much that Cloud Identity

00:23:49.410 --> 00:23:52.070
is the key driver for our
digital transformation,

00:23:52.070 --> 00:23:53.210
Google is.

00:23:53.210 --> 00:23:57.230
And we went to Cloud Identity
because it's your identity

00:23:57.230 --> 00:24:01.940
product, not because we went
through an exhaustive search

00:24:01.940 --> 00:24:03.920
and soul searching,
decided it was best for us.

00:24:03.920 --> 00:24:04.798
It's your product.

00:24:04.798 --> 00:24:06.590
We're pushing everybody
onto your products.

00:24:06.590 --> 00:24:08.790
We're pushing all our
stuff onto your cloud.

00:24:08.790 --> 00:24:13.880
And we are working with Google
to work through all the kinks

00:24:13.880 --> 00:24:14.580
to make it work.

00:24:17.290 --> 00:24:18.880
Cost savings--
cost savings there

00:24:18.880 --> 00:24:21.367
is your enterprise agreement.

00:24:21.367 --> 00:24:23.450
Right now, we're carrying
the enterprise agreement

00:24:23.450 --> 00:24:24.310
for Microsoft.

00:24:24.310 --> 00:24:27.220
And your-- I don't know.

00:24:27.220 --> 00:24:29.950
What do you call the
Google licensing agreement?

00:24:29.950 --> 00:24:32.230
But we're carrying both.

00:24:32.230 --> 00:24:34.830
We will not save a penny
until we kill one of them.

00:24:34.830 --> 00:24:39.270
And that one of them is
the enterprise agreement.

00:24:39.270 --> 00:24:43.800
OK, challenges-- I think
all I've talked about

00:24:43.800 --> 00:24:45.290
is challenges so far.

00:24:45.290 --> 00:24:48.970
But again, single
source of truth.

00:24:48.970 --> 00:24:51.860
We have an HR system which,
through the magic of PowerShell

00:24:51.860 --> 00:24:55.390
scripts, tells us who
joined and who left.

00:24:55.390 --> 00:24:59.270
And we push that
through AD, and then use

00:24:59.270 --> 00:25:03.170
that to kill access in all
of these downstream systems.

00:25:03.170 --> 00:25:06.340
And it doesn't really work.

00:25:06.340 --> 00:25:07.468
It works most of the time.

00:25:07.468 --> 00:25:09.760
But if an auditor finds one
guy in an audit, that's it.

00:25:09.760 --> 00:25:10.570
I failed the audit.

00:25:10.570 --> 00:25:15.160
IT general controls audit is
a high finding against me.

00:25:15.160 --> 00:25:18.490
Legacy apps-- we have
legacy going back to the day

00:25:18.490 --> 00:25:19.510
Tony bought the airline.

00:25:19.510 --> 00:25:21.820
Because it's an airline,
so you are always

00:25:21.820 --> 00:25:24.280
carrying applications
which are--

00:25:24.280 --> 00:25:28.390
you have a illegal compliance to
use an application which works

00:25:28.390 --> 00:25:30.310
on a particular technology set.

00:25:30.310 --> 00:25:32.560
And if that vendor
says, I don't support,

00:25:32.560 --> 00:25:35.290
then he doesn't support.

00:25:35.290 --> 00:25:37.040
And we have to keep
using the application,

00:25:37.040 --> 00:25:38.748
so we need something
to fill in that gap.

00:25:42.000 --> 00:25:46.740
PaperCut-- I would rather we
didn't use PaperCut by just

00:25:46.740 --> 00:25:49.420
killing all the printers.

00:25:49.420 --> 00:25:51.730
But I'm not allowed to do that.

00:25:51.730 --> 00:25:55.560
So we do need
something to allow--

00:25:55.560 --> 00:25:57.270
we have a FollowMe printing now.

00:25:57.270 --> 00:25:59.340
And we need something
that allows that to work.

00:25:59.340 --> 00:26:03.500
And PaperCut is a good
product, and it does that well.

00:26:03.500 --> 00:26:05.700
But heads up, men.

00:26:05.700 --> 00:26:07.170
I want to kill paper.

00:26:07.170 --> 00:26:11.040
So once I get killing all of
this legacy Microsoft stuff,

00:26:11.040 --> 00:26:13.298
we'll be going after the paper.

00:26:13.298 --> 00:26:15.090
But I think I've been
saying that for about

00:26:15.090 --> 00:26:17.040
10 years in companies
I've worked for,

00:26:17.040 --> 00:26:19.380
and we still buy printers.

00:26:19.380 --> 00:26:21.260
SPEAKER 3: That's
actually our goal, too.

00:26:21.260 --> 00:26:22.310
DECLAN HOGAN: Yeah?

00:26:22.310 --> 00:26:23.280
OK, good.

00:26:23.280 --> 00:26:23.780
Good.

00:26:27.810 --> 00:26:29.820
OK, that is the ultimate goal.

00:26:29.820 --> 00:26:35.390
So yeah, AD, it's like Rasputin.

00:26:35.390 --> 00:26:37.880
You keep putting bullets
in it, but we can never

00:26:37.880 --> 00:26:39.540
actually kill it.

00:26:39.540 --> 00:26:40.040
[LAUGHTER]

00:26:40.040 --> 00:26:43.360
And it's a race to kill
it before it kills me.

00:26:43.360 --> 00:26:45.740
[LAUGHTER]

00:26:45.740 --> 00:26:49.790
And that's why we picked--
we picked Google, not pick--

00:26:49.790 --> 00:26:50.920
well, I hate this.

00:26:50.920 --> 00:26:52.230
Sorry, Google recording this.

00:26:52.230 --> 00:26:53.772
But we did not pick
Google because we

00:26:53.772 --> 00:26:56.210
thought it was the most mature
and the best product set.

00:26:56.210 --> 00:26:58.520
And the whole
company cringed at me

00:26:58.520 --> 00:27:02.300
when I started making them
use G Drive and G Suite.

00:27:02.300 --> 00:27:06.020
But we found that
Microsoft did not

00:27:06.020 --> 00:27:09.170
care about making Active
Directory that much better.

00:27:09.170 --> 00:27:11.810
They did not care about
my general controls

00:27:11.810 --> 00:27:13.040
audit findings.

00:27:13.040 --> 00:27:15.470
They cared about selling
me their licenses

00:27:15.470 --> 00:27:17.480
or selling us their licenses.

00:27:17.480 --> 00:27:22.340
And Google, they will come
and say, yeah, you know what?

00:27:22.340 --> 00:27:24.980
Our product is not
that mature now.

00:27:24.980 --> 00:27:27.890
They know they're fighting
against AD in just 99%

00:27:27.890 --> 00:27:31.880
of all companies, but
they are willing to fill

00:27:31.880 --> 00:27:34.468
in the gap between
where you're at now.

00:27:34.468 --> 00:27:36.260
And even we will give
them systems where we

00:27:36.260 --> 00:27:37.468
don't have any documentation.

00:27:37.468 --> 00:27:39.230
We don't know what
the system does.

00:27:39.230 --> 00:27:42.180
And sometimes, we don't
even know where it's hosted.

00:27:42.180 --> 00:27:46.250
But we just give it to Google,
give it to their partners.

00:27:46.250 --> 00:27:49.580
And Edward there, he's just
knocking over those systems one

00:27:49.580 --> 00:27:50.220
at a time.

00:27:50.220 --> 00:27:52.320
How many have we
done so far, Edward?

00:27:52.320 --> 00:27:53.190
20 or so?

00:27:53.190 --> 00:27:53.690
More?

00:27:53.690 --> 00:27:55.420
EDWARD: Yeah, it's
more than 20 now.

00:27:55.420 --> 00:27:58.850
DECLAN HOGAN: Yeah,
so we're maybe

00:27:58.850 --> 00:28:02.070
20%, maybe a quarter of the way
through where we need to be.

00:28:02.070 --> 00:28:04.112
And we have to get there
by the end of this year.

00:28:06.920 --> 00:28:09.560
Is this our environment, Edward?

00:28:09.560 --> 00:28:11.085
This is the cleanest--

00:28:11.085 --> 00:28:13.310
[LAUGHTER]

00:28:13.310 --> 00:28:17.960
--version of our environment
that I have ever seen.

00:28:17.960 --> 00:28:18.760
Wow.

00:28:18.760 --> 00:28:21.010
RAVI KIRAN KUMAR: We put a
little [INAUDIBLE] on that,

00:28:21.010 --> 00:28:22.400
so it shined up a little bit.

00:28:22.400 --> 00:28:23.495
DECLAN HOGAN: Wow, yeah.

00:28:23.495 --> 00:28:25.370
If we look like this,
we don't need you guys.

00:28:25.370 --> 00:28:26.950
[LAUGHTER]

00:28:27.720 --> 00:28:29.220
We'll stick with AD.

00:28:29.220 --> 00:28:33.220
But yeah, it's a hell of
a lot messier than that.

00:28:33.220 --> 00:28:35.540
And there's even
applications in there

00:28:35.540 --> 00:28:39.310
which we don't go through AD,
we just go here and log in.

00:28:39.310 --> 00:28:42.470
We have a single sign-on
system that we custom

00:28:42.470 --> 00:28:46.760
built many, many
years before my time.

00:28:46.760 --> 00:28:49.443
And sometimes, it talks to AD.

00:28:49.443 --> 00:28:50.360
Sometimes, it doesn't.

00:28:50.360 --> 00:28:52.820
Some systems are just too
old-school that we just

00:28:52.820 --> 00:28:55.200
say, here user, go and
figure out how to log in.

00:28:55.200 --> 00:28:56.350
Manage two passwords.

00:28:56.350 --> 00:29:00.020
We don't care if it never
expires, just work it out.

00:29:00.020 --> 00:29:03.660
But that's the nicest item I've
ever seen of our environment.

00:29:03.660 --> 00:29:06.080
RAVI KIRAN KUMAR: And in
many cases, what we see is

00:29:06.080 --> 00:29:09.133
it's even hard to know how
many applications you have.

00:29:09.133 --> 00:29:10.800
So when we talk to
our customers-- like,

00:29:10.800 --> 00:29:13.950
hey, tell us how many
applications are talking to AD.

00:29:13.950 --> 00:29:14.960
They'll say, good luck.

00:29:14.960 --> 00:29:17.120
So I'll come back
after three months.

00:29:17.120 --> 00:29:19.755
So because there's no
application inventory

00:29:19.755 --> 00:29:21.380
management and things
like that, so you

00:29:21.380 --> 00:29:24.470
have these applications
floating around in spreadsheets

00:29:24.470 --> 00:29:25.500
all over.

00:29:25.500 --> 00:29:27.710
And then you have
these spreadsheets

00:29:27.710 --> 00:29:30.048
with application owners
and then individual admins.

00:29:30.048 --> 00:29:30.590
So that's it.

00:29:30.590 --> 00:29:31.340
DECLAN HOGAN: Yep.

00:29:31.340 --> 00:29:33.440
And the boys over
there will tell you we

00:29:33.440 --> 00:29:36.730
restructure every six months.

00:29:36.730 --> 00:29:39.455
And departments come and go.

00:29:39.455 --> 00:29:41.080
They go off and be
their own companies.

00:29:41.080 --> 00:29:42.705
And then they come
back into the group,

00:29:42.705 --> 00:29:44.260
and then they go back out again.

00:29:44.260 --> 00:29:47.050
And we're having to
change domain names for--

00:29:47.050 --> 00:29:50.650
let's say we've spun off
our ground handling company

00:29:50.650 --> 00:29:55.570
into a separate company and
a JV with a Singapore ground

00:29:55.570 --> 00:29:56.440
handler.

00:29:56.440 --> 00:30:01.362
So overnight, 5,000 staff
worked for a different company.

00:30:01.362 --> 00:30:03.820
They still need to authenticate
back into our applications,

00:30:03.820 --> 00:30:06.160
because they're doing the
ground handling for us.

00:30:06.160 --> 00:30:11.650
They had @airasia.com email
addresses at midnight one

00:30:11.650 --> 00:30:17.332
night, and then at 12:01
they became @gtr.com.

00:30:17.332 --> 00:30:20.920
They still have
all our hardware.

00:30:20.920 --> 00:30:22.300
They're still doing work for us.

00:30:22.300 --> 00:30:25.340
They're still wearing shirts
with the AirAsia logo on it,

00:30:25.340 --> 00:30:28.840
but they're a separate
company, separate HR system,

00:30:28.840 --> 00:30:31.570
separate payroll,
separate policies.

00:30:31.570 --> 00:30:37.020
And we have to manage that
happening quite a lot.

00:30:37.020 --> 00:30:41.130
And that's happened
over the last 10 years.

00:30:41.130 --> 00:30:43.950
So when people leave
those other companies,

00:30:43.950 --> 00:30:45.640
we don't always know about it.

00:30:45.640 --> 00:30:49.320
So we're relying on--
and so sometimes,

00:30:49.320 --> 00:30:52.590
it's even the HR person has to
send an Excel spreadsheet to us

00:30:52.590 --> 00:30:54.030
that we have to then load.

00:30:54.030 --> 00:30:55.710
And you give that
to an auditor, it's

00:30:55.710 --> 00:30:57.970
like shooting fish in a barrel.

00:30:57.970 --> 00:31:00.140
So yeah, but nice diagram.

00:31:00.140 --> 00:31:01.520
I wish it was like this.

00:31:04.350 --> 00:31:06.191
OK, this is getting
more like it now.

00:31:06.191 --> 00:31:08.350
[LAUGHTER]

00:31:08.350 --> 00:31:11.640
OK, so we're going
to kill all of that.

00:31:14.190 --> 00:31:17.510
CRM system is Salesforce.

00:31:17.510 --> 00:31:19.730
HR system is Workday.

00:31:19.730 --> 00:31:22.420
So Salesforce, no problem.

00:31:22.420 --> 00:31:26.260
Workday, problem
now, but soon to

00:31:26.260 --> 00:31:28.822
be not a problem because
we're working hard.

00:31:28.822 --> 00:31:30.280
We find that we
spend a lot of time

00:31:30.280 --> 00:31:35.560
bullying the third-party
vendors to talk to Google.

00:31:35.560 --> 00:31:38.290
Google are very good at
saying, OK, we'll do it.

00:31:38.290 --> 00:31:40.240
Please try and make
your vendor do it.

00:31:40.240 --> 00:31:44.090
So Workday are one
of those vendors.

00:31:44.090 --> 00:31:46.490
So technology is ready.

00:31:46.490 --> 00:31:47.770
It's contractual.

00:31:47.770 --> 00:31:50.770
We're waiting for
contractual issues

00:31:50.770 --> 00:31:52.570
to be resolved
before we can just

00:31:52.570 --> 00:31:54.160
hook it through Cloud Identity.

00:31:54.160 --> 00:31:56.480
And we find that with the
smaller vendors where we're

00:31:56.480 --> 00:31:57.730
their biggest client, it's OK.

00:31:57.730 --> 00:31:58.480
We can bully them.

00:31:58.480 --> 00:32:00.700
But for the big guys, yeah,
we kind of need Google.

00:32:00.700 --> 00:32:02.740
And having Google
as a partner who

00:32:02.740 --> 00:32:05.890
are willing to do that for us,
that works really well for us.

00:32:08.900 --> 00:32:12.062
OK, App Engine-- so the
dev team are happily

00:32:12.062 --> 00:32:13.270
building stuff on App Engine.

00:32:13.270 --> 00:32:16.885
So that stuff gets hooked
in fairly natively.

00:32:20.140 --> 00:32:21.490
VPNs-- again, yeah.

00:32:21.490 --> 00:32:24.560
This stuff is fairly standard.

00:32:24.560 --> 00:32:27.120
We are moving everybody off
those Windows environments,

00:32:27.120 --> 00:32:27.920
again, on desktops.

00:32:27.920 --> 00:32:29.545
We are pushing people
onto Chromebooks.

00:32:29.545 --> 00:32:30.890
Again, more people are crying.

00:32:30.890 --> 00:32:35.480
But we just bribe them with
Pixelbooks, and they like it.

00:32:35.480 --> 00:32:38.540
But once that Microsoft
enterprise agreement goes,

00:32:38.540 --> 00:32:40.520
all of those Windows
licenses for those people

00:32:40.520 --> 00:32:42.630
to sign into those
machines will disappear.

00:32:42.630 --> 00:32:47.090
So we will need an
emulator to allow

00:32:47.090 --> 00:32:49.280
Wintel tech at the
back end hosted

00:32:49.280 --> 00:32:52.550
on GCP to talk to a user.

00:32:52.550 --> 00:32:55.850
We will use
[? Suspass ?] and Citrix.

00:32:55.850 --> 00:32:58.860
We will have RDS licenses,
which we'll still

00:32:58.860 --> 00:33:02.780
need that RDS piece to talk
to that back-end Wintel.

00:33:02.780 --> 00:33:05.330
We will either just consume
that license from Microsoft

00:33:05.330 --> 00:33:08.180
ourselves, or we will build it
into Citrix and [? Suspass. ?]

00:33:08.180 --> 00:33:11.150
And we will use [? Suspass ?]
as well to do the single sign-on

00:33:11.150 --> 00:33:13.610
and the password
management piece.

00:33:13.610 --> 00:33:17.510
And eventually, we have to pick
Citrix because of one legacy

00:33:17.510 --> 00:33:20.752
or one of our most
important applications.

00:33:20.752 --> 00:33:24.530
It's too important for us to
do it while trying to make it

00:33:24.530 --> 00:33:27.335
work on [? Suspass, ?] so
we need Citrix for that.

00:33:27.335 --> 00:33:29.270
A little bit of complexity,
but not too bad.

00:33:31.820 --> 00:33:35.840
OK, benefits-- so yeah,
we're going everything

00:33:35.840 --> 00:33:38.540
on GCP, absolutely everything.

00:33:38.540 --> 00:33:41.630
The one caveat is China.

00:33:41.630 --> 00:33:51.150
So 20% of our passengers and
revenue comes out of China.

00:33:51.150 --> 00:33:54.480
I won't ask any Google people to
comment on their China strategy

00:33:54.480 --> 00:34:02.460
here, but our issue is
more on bad actors in China

00:34:02.460 --> 00:34:05.880
coming at us and trying to
hold inventory on our website.

00:34:05.880 --> 00:34:10.590
So anybody else here
work for an airline?

00:34:10.590 --> 00:34:12.120
No?

00:34:12.120 --> 00:34:15.750
Well, the problem for
us is that in order

00:34:15.750 --> 00:34:19.170
for us to sell you a seat on the
airline, we have to make sure

00:34:19.170 --> 00:34:22.897
we hold that inventory
before you give us the money.

00:34:22.897 --> 00:34:24.480
And because that
inventory is finite--

00:34:24.480 --> 00:34:28.099
there's only 180
seats on a 320--

00:34:28.099 --> 00:34:34.380
a bot will come along, hold that
entry, pass us a fake payment,

00:34:34.380 --> 00:34:36.527
hold that seat for as long
as the bank comes back

00:34:36.527 --> 00:34:37.860
and tell the payment is refused.

00:34:37.860 --> 00:34:38.902
So that might be seconds.

00:34:38.902 --> 00:34:39.810
It might be minutes.

00:34:39.810 --> 00:34:42.870
We will wait up to 15 minutes
for the bank to respond.

00:34:42.870 --> 00:34:47.100
And they will hit a
plane, one flight,

00:34:47.100 --> 00:34:49.012
tens of thousands
of times, dominating

00:34:49.012 --> 00:34:51.179
all of that inventory and
not allowing a real person

00:34:51.179 --> 00:34:53.460
to actually buy the seat.

00:34:53.460 --> 00:34:56.100
They will take that
inventory, advertise it

00:34:56.100 --> 00:35:00.660
on a third-party website,
like an OTA site in China.

00:35:00.660 --> 00:35:03.000
So if they're
holding it at $100,

00:35:03.000 --> 00:35:05.670
they will advertise it at $150.

00:35:05.670 --> 00:35:08.730
They will wait until
somebody gives them $150,

00:35:08.730 --> 00:35:10.383
then they will give us $100.

00:35:10.383 --> 00:35:12.300
And then four hours
before the flight departs,

00:35:12.300 --> 00:35:14.217
they will drop any
inventory that they haven't

00:35:14.217 --> 00:35:15.860
held because they can't sell.

00:35:15.860 --> 00:35:18.360
We have to legally stop selling
four hours before the flight

00:35:18.360 --> 00:35:18.860
departs.

00:35:22.170 --> 00:35:26.260
And we've been having this
problem for two years,

00:35:26.260 --> 00:35:28.870
maybe longer--
[? Sadesh, ?] longer--

00:35:28.870 --> 00:35:33.000
where a flight will look
like it's got no seats left.

00:35:33.000 --> 00:35:36.270
They'll all look sold or
held, but it will take off

00:35:36.270 --> 00:35:38.260
with the flight half empty.

00:35:38.260 --> 00:35:40.560
And that's a massive
hit on our revenue.

00:35:40.560 --> 00:35:44.310
And those bad guys are
coming from inside China,

00:35:44.310 --> 00:35:47.100
so we need a WAF inside China
that can see the traffic

00:35:47.100 --> 00:35:48.630
right from the origin.

00:35:48.630 --> 00:35:52.260
So until Google get
their armor product

00:35:52.260 --> 00:35:59.850
to be a real AI-driven WAF
tool, not just an IP blacklist,

00:35:59.850 --> 00:36:05.050
then, yeah, we're
stuck on Alibaba.

00:36:05.050 --> 00:36:06.840
Ease of management--
I think I've

00:36:06.840 --> 00:36:09.480
described how we currently
manage through AD,

00:36:09.480 --> 00:36:10.530
and that's not very easy.

00:36:13.610 --> 00:36:17.620
So we want that single pane
of glass, single console,

00:36:17.620 --> 00:36:19.240
everything managed through it.

00:36:19.240 --> 00:36:20.980
And we want GUIs like that.

00:36:20.980 --> 00:36:25.090
I don't want to be having to
dive down into physical AD

00:36:25.090 --> 00:36:27.460
boxes in five
different countries

00:36:27.460 --> 00:36:31.450
to see why a profile
exists in one place that's

00:36:31.450 --> 00:36:33.060
supposed to be gone.

00:36:33.060 --> 00:36:36.640
And that's just a nightmare now.

00:36:36.640 --> 00:36:39.790
Security concerns-- yeah,
the security concerns are--

00:36:39.790 --> 00:36:42.580
because we keep having these
IT general controls issues,

00:36:42.580 --> 00:36:46.165
all of those people,
if they're in AD,

00:36:46.165 --> 00:36:47.290
they're inside the network.

00:36:47.290 --> 00:36:49.248
If they're inside the
network, they have access

00:36:49.248 --> 00:36:52.060
to data and systems.

00:36:52.060 --> 00:36:56.500
And if they are bad actors
and through omission by us

00:36:56.500 --> 00:36:59.890
they have access, then
our data is insecure.

00:36:59.890 --> 00:37:02.830
We cannot have that happen
in this day and age.

00:37:02.830 --> 00:37:05.440
So that needs to
be fixed this year.

00:37:05.440 --> 00:37:07.480
And cost saving-- simple.

00:37:07.480 --> 00:37:09.100
Just kill that
enterprise agreement.

00:37:09.100 --> 00:37:12.450
[MUSIC PLAYING]

